<div class="ux-article"><details class="article-disclosure"><summary class="article-toggle"><span>INTERACTIVE ARTICLE</span><span class="article-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><nav class="article-nav" aria-label="Article contents"><a href="#problem">The problem</a><a href="#analysis">The analysis</a><a href="#refinement">The refinement</a><a href="#evaluation">The evidence</a></nav><div class="article-grid"><div class="section-heading" id="problem"><h2>Creating interfaces is easy. Understanding them still takes people.</h2></div><div class="annotated-paragraph"><p>We can generate another interface in seconds. But can people find what they need, understand what to do next, and accomplish their goals? Producing more alternatives does not answer those questions.</p><aside class="margin-note">Human studies, interviews, and interaction data reveal experiences that a simulation can only approximate.</aside></div><p>Human evaluation remains essential, yet it takes time and resources. Simulated users offer another source of feedback during early design iterations: give browser agents a goal and a persona, then let them explore an interface.</p><figure class="wide gap-figure" data-animation><div class="gap-scene"><div class="prototype-stack"><img class="prototype p0" src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/prototype-1.svg" alt="" /><img class="prototype p1" src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/prototype-2.svg" alt="" /><img class="prototype p2" src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/prototype-3.svg" alt="" /><span class="diagram-label">More interfaces</span></div><div class="gap-questions"><span>Can users find what they need?</span><span>Is the next action clear?</span><span>Can users complete their goal?</span></div><div class="human-focus"><span class="diagram-label">Human experience</span><div class="human-evidence"><div><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/user-studies.svg" alt="" /><span>User studies</span></div><div><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/interviews.svg" alt="" /><span>Interviews</span></div><div><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/interaction-analytics.svg" alt="" /><span>Interaction data</span></div></div></div></div><div class="motion-controls"><button type="button" data-motion aria-label="Pause animation" title="Pause animation">Ⅱ</button></div></figure><p>However, simulation introduces a problem of its own. Many agents produce many action logs, explanations, and potential issues. A designer still has to work out what matters, where it happened, and what to do about it.</p><p><strong>UXCascade addresses this analysis problem.</strong> It structures agent-generated feedback into a workflow that moves from goals and persona patterns to concrete issues and possible improvements.</p><p>The illustration below uses invented reasoning excerpts to show how goals and possible issues are extracted, linked to configured persona traits, and organized across runs. Open an excerpt to inspect its reasoning.</p><figure class="wide trace-figure"><div class="reasoning-demo" data-trace-stage="raw"><div class="trace-toolbar"><span class="mini-label">VERBOSE OUTPUT → LINKED RECORDS → ORGANIZED FINDINGS</span><button type="button" id="play-processing" aria-label="Play transformation" title="Pause or play transformation">▶</button></div><div class="process-stages" role="group" aria-label="Trace processing stage"><button type="button" data-trace="raw" aria-pressed="true"><b>Raw reasoning</b><span>Read the agent output</span></button><button type="button" data-trace="extract" aria-pressed="false"><b>Extract & link</b><span>Goals + existing persona traits + issues</span></button><button type="button" data-trace="organize" aria-pressed="false"><b>Organize findings</b><span>Group related records</span></button></div><div class="trace-viewport" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Agent reasoning and extracted records"><div class="raw-records"><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:0"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A01</b> I have opened the Purchase page and can see a collection of font cards. Alaska looks suitable, and the price is visible, but I<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">I have opened the Purchase page and can see a collection of font cards. Alaska looks suitable, and the price is visible, but I cannot see a control for adding it to my cart. I will look underneath the card and scroll down before deciding whether the whole card is clickable. I want to complete the purchase without opening several unrelated pages.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>new visitor · time pressure</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Purchase a font</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A01</dt><dd>new visitor · time pressure</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>Purchase controls hard to find</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:1"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A02</b> The collection is listed at CHF 400, while the individual font card shows CHF 100. I need to understand what each amount includes before<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">The collection is listed at CHF 400, while the individual font card shows CHF 100. I need to understand what each amount includes before choosing. The page mentions styles and glyphs, but that does not tell me whether the listed price covers the license I need. I will inspect another card and look for an explanation of the collection price.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>returning visitor · budget conscious</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Compare prices</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A02</dt><dd>returning visitor · budget conscious</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>Price scope unclear</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:2"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A03</b> I can read the font name and inspect its appearance, but I do not yet know which license applies to my intended use. There<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">I can read the font name and inspect its appearance, but I do not yet know which license applies to my intended use. There is some introductory purchase text, and I may need to enter the number of licenses farther down. I will search the page for licensing information before adding anything, because I do not want to choose an unsuitable option.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>new visitor · unhurried</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Understand licensing</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A03</dt><dd>new visitor · unhurried</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>License guidance hard to locate</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:3"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A04</b> I have found the font I came for. The card gives me a price and a specimen, but I am still looking for the<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">I have found the font I came for. The card gives me a price and a specimen, but I am still looking for the purchase action. I expected a button near the price. I will scroll a little farther to check whether the controls are below the current viewport rather than assume that clicking the specimen starts the purchase.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>returning visitor · time pressure</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Purchase a font</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A04</dt><dd>returning visitor · time pressure</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>Purchase controls hard to find</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:4"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A05</b> I am comparing the collection with buying a single font. The two prices are easy to spot, yet I cannot determine whether I am<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">I am comparing the collection with buying a single font. The two prices are easy to spot, yet I cannot determine whether I am comparing the same license quantities. I will revisit the purchase instructions and look for a breakdown. Until I understand what is included, I do not have enough information to decide which option fits my budget.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>new visitor · budget conscious</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Compare prices</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A05</dt><dd>new visitor · budget conscious</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>Price scope unclear</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:5"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A06</b> I recognize the font family and know which style I want. The remaining question is the license. I can see purchase information, but I<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">I recognize the font family and know which style I want. The remaining question is the license. I can see purchase information, but I have not found a clear explanation of where the font may be used. I will inspect the surrounding links and the lower portion of the page before choosing a quantity or continuing with the order.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>returning visitor · unhurried</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Understand licensing</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A06</dt><dd>returning visitor · unhurried</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>License guidance hard to locate</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:6"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A07</b> The Alaska card is visually prominent, so I have paused here to inspect it. I can read CHF 100 but cannot find an explicit<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">The Alaska card is visually prominent, so I have paused here to inspect it. I can read CHF 100 but cannot find an explicit Add to Cart action in this view. I could try clicking the card, although I do not know whether that opens a specimen or starts an order. I will first scroll to find a more explicit purchase control.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>new visitor · unhurried</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Purchase a font</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A07</dt><dd>new visitor · unhurried</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>Purchase controls hard to find</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:7"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A08</b> I am considering whether the complete collection is better value than the individual typeface. I see both prices, but the labels do not explain<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">I am considering whether the complete collection is better value than the individual typeface. I see both prices, but the labels do not explain the license assumptions well enough for me to compare them confidently. I will look for a description next to the collection heading, then check the purchase options before deciding which item to add.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>returning visitor · budget conscious</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Compare prices</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A08</dt><dd>returning visitor · budget conscious</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>Price scope unclear</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:8"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A09</b> I want to find out whether I can use this font for my project before committing to a purchase. The page has attractive specimens,<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">I want to find out whether I can use this font for my project before committing to a purchase. The page has attractive specimens, but the licensing answer is not apparent in the area I am viewing. I will scan the page headings and links for a license section. If I cannot locate it, I will need to leave this purchase flow to investigate.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>new visitor · time pressure</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Understand licensing</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A09</dt><dd>new visitor · time pressure</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>License guidance hard to locate</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:9"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A10</b> I have selected the typeface mentally and am ready to continue. The price suggests that this is the correct product card, yet there is<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">I have selected the typeface mentally and am ready to continue. The price suggests that this is the correct product card, yet there is no visible control that clearly adds it to the cart. I will move down the page and check for quantity and purchase controls. I want to see what will be added before taking an action that might change my order.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>returning visitor · unhurried</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Purchase a font</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A10</dt><dd>returning visitor · unhurried</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>Purchase controls hard to find</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:10"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A11</b> I need to make a quick price comparison between the collection and the individual typeface. The prices alone do not make the difference in<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">I need to make a quick price comparison between the collection and the individual typeface. The prices alone do not make the difference in included options clear. I will check whether selecting the card reveals a price breakdown, and then return to the collection heading if necessary. At the moment I am comparing amounts without knowing whether their coverage is equivalent.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>new visitor · time pressure</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Compare prices</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A11</dt><dd>new visitor · time pressure</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>Price scope unclear</dd></div></dl></article><article class="reasoning-record" style="--record-index:11"><div class="record-source"><details class="reasoning-memory"><summary><b class="record-id">A12</b> Before I choose a license quantity, I need to know what the purchase permits. I can identify the typeface and its price, but the<span class="memory-ellipsis" aria-label="Expand reasoning">...</span></summary><p class="reasoning-text">Before I choose a license quantity, I need to know what the purchase permits. I can identify the typeface and its price, but the connection between that price and the licensing terms is not visible here. I will search for the license explanation and then revisit the product card. Otherwise I may choose an option that does not match the project requirements.</p></details><div class="original-traits"><span>CONFIGURED TRAITS</span>returning visitor · budget conscious</div></div><span class="extract-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><dl class="extracted-fields"><div><dt>Goal / extracted</dt><dd>Understand licensing</dd></div><div><dt>Traits / linked from A12</dt><dd>returning visitor · budget conscious</dd></div><div><dt>Possible issue / identified</dt><dd>License guidance hard to locate</dd></div></dl></article></div><div class="organized-records" hidden><div class="organization-key"><span>GOAL</span><span>PERSONA TRAITS + SOURCE</span><span>POSSIBLE ISSUE</span></div><section class="organized-group"><h3>Purchase a font<small>4 illustrative records</small></h3><div class="linked-personas"><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A01</b> new visitor · time pressure</summary><p>I have opened the Purchase page and can see a collection of font cards. Alaska looks suitable, and the price is visible, but I cannot see a control for adding it to my cart. I will look underneath the card and scroll down before deciding whether the whole card is clickable. I want to complete the purchase without opening several unrelated pages.</p></details><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A04</b> returning visitor · time pressure</summary><p>I have found the font I came for. The card gives me a price and a specimen, but I am still looking for the purchase action. I expected a button near the price. I will scroll a little farther to check whether the controls are below the current viewport rather than assume that clicking the specimen starts the purchase.</p></details><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A07</b> new visitor · unhurried</summary><p>The Alaska card is visually prominent, so I have paused here to inspect it. I can read CHF 100 but cannot find an explicit Add to Cart action in this view. I could try clicking the card, although I do not know whether that opens a specimen or starts an order. I will first scroll to find a more explicit purchase control.</p></details><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A10</b> returning visitor · unhurried</summary><p>I have selected the typeface mentally and am ready to continue. The price suggests that this is the correct product card, yet there is no visible control that clearly adds it to the cart. I will move down the page and check for quantity and purchase controls. I want to see what will be added before taking an action that might change my order.</p></details></div><div class="group-issue"><span>↳</span><strong>Purchase controls hard to find</strong><small>Linked to A01, A04, A07, A10</small></div></section><section class="organized-group"><h3>Compare prices<small>4 illustrative records</small></h3><div class="linked-personas"><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A02</b> returning visitor · budget conscious</summary><p>The collection is listed at CHF 400, while the individual font card shows CHF 100. I need to understand what each amount includes before choosing. The page mentions styles and glyphs, but that does not tell me whether the listed price covers the license I need. I will inspect another card and look for an explanation of the collection price.</p></details><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A05</b> new visitor · budget conscious</summary><p>I am comparing the collection with buying a single font. The two prices are easy to spot, yet I cannot determine whether I am comparing the same license quantities. I will revisit the purchase instructions and look for a breakdown. Until I understand what is included, I do not have enough information to decide which option fits my budget.</p></details><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A08</b> returning visitor · budget conscious</summary><p>I am considering whether the complete collection is better value than the individual typeface. I see both prices, but the labels do not explain the license assumptions well enough for me to compare them confidently. I will look for a description next to the collection heading, then check the purchase options before deciding which item to add.</p></details><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A11</b> new visitor · time pressure</summary><p>I need to make a quick price comparison between the collection and the individual typeface. The prices alone do not make the difference in included options clear. I will check whether selecting the card reveals a price breakdown, and then return to the collection heading if necessary. At the moment I am comparing amounts without knowing whether their coverage is equivalent.</p></details></div><div class="group-issue"><span>↳</span><strong>Price scope unclear</strong><small>Linked to A02, A05, A08, A11</small></div></section><section class="organized-group"><h3>Understand licensing<small>4 illustrative records</small></h3><div class="linked-personas"><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A03</b> new visitor · unhurried</summary><p>I can read the font name and inspect its appearance, but I do not yet know which license applies to my intended use. There is some introductory purchase text, and I may need to enter the number of licenses farther down. I will search the page for licensing information before adding anything, because I do not want to choose an unsuitable option.</p></details><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A06</b> returning visitor · unhurried</summary><p>I recognize the font family and know which style I want. The remaining question is the license. I can see purchase information, but I have not found a clear explanation of where the font may be used. I will inspect the surrounding links and the lower portion of the page before choosing a quantity or continuing with the order.</p></details><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A09</b> new visitor · time pressure</summary><p>I want to find out whether I can use this font for my project before committing to a purchase. The page has attractive specimens, but the licensing answer is not apparent in the area I am viewing. I will scan the page headings and links for a license section. If I cannot locate it, I will need to leave this purchase flow to investigate.</p></details><details class="source-record"><summary><b>A12</b> returning visitor · budget conscious</summary><p>Before I choose a license quantity, I need to know what the purchase permits. I can identify the typeface and its price, but the connection between that price and the licensing terms is not visible here. I will search for the license explanation and then revisit the product card. Otherwise I may choose an option that does not match the project requirements.</p></details></div><div class="group-issue"><span>↳</span><strong>License guidance hard to locate</strong><small>Linked to A03, A06, A09, A12</small></div></section></div></div><div class="processing-footer"><p id="trace-status" aria-live="polite">Agents produce long explanations. The goals and possible issues are still embedded in the prose.</p></div></div></figure><details class="technical"><summary>Why these entry points? The formative study<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-body"><p>We interviewed five UX professionals. Their needs became five requirements: support frequent iteration, provide access to different perspectives, connect insights to goals, generate feedback on demand, and help prioritize findings.</p><div class="requirements"><div><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/iterative.svg" alt="" /><span><b>Iteration</b>Fit the pace of early design.</span></div><div><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/perspectives.svg" alt="" /><span><b>Perspectives</b>Explore persona-aligned feedback.</span></div><div><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/goal-aligned.svg" alt="" /><span><b>Goals</b>Connect breakdowns to user intentions.</span></div><div><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/on-demand.svg" alt="" /><span><b>On demand</b>Generate and revisit feedback.</span></div><div><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/prioritize-findings.svg" alt="" /><span><b>Prioritization</b>Find actionable issues in many traces.</span></div></div><p>These requirements informed a workflow with three analysis stages—Explore, Observe, Isolate—and two refinement stages—Propose, Evaluate.</p></div></details><div class="section-heading" id="analysis"><h2>Start with a pattern. Follow it to the evidence.</h2></div><div class="annotated-paragraph"><p>The analysis begins with the goals agents pursued and their outcomes. Choosing a goal reveals distributions across persona traits. Selecting a trait or persona then narrows the view to concrete issues, each connected to its explanation, interface context, and place in the journey.</p><aside class="margin-note">Traits describe how agents were configured. A pattern in simulated behavior is a lead to investigate, not evidence that real people with that trait behave the same way.</aside></div><figure class="wide analysis-figure" data-animation><div class="workflow-path"><div class="workflow-stop stop-1"><span class="workflow-circle"><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/workflow-1.svg" alt="Step 1" /></span><div><h3>Explore</h3><p>Goals & outcomes</p></div></div><div class="workflow-stop stop-2"><span class="workflow-circle"><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/workflow-2.svg" alt="Step 2" /></span><div><h3>Observe</h3><p>Trait distributions</p></div></div><div class="workflow-stop stop-3"><span class="workflow-circle"><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/unified/workflow-3.svg" alt="Step 3" /></span><div><h3>Isolate</h3><p>UX issues & reasons</p></div></div><div class="workflow-return"><span>↳</span> Propose a fix <b>→</b> Evaluate a step <b>↺</b></div></div><div class="motion-controls"><button type="button" data-motion aria-label="Pause animation" title="Pause animation">Ⅱ</button></div></figure><p>Consider the paper’s font-website example. A visitor is trying to purchase a font, but the add-to-cart and license-detail controls are not visible in the current viewport. The agent’s next action is to scroll.</p><p>The issue is not presented as a detached verdict. It is connected to <strong>the controls, the agent’s explanation, and journey step 16</strong>. A practitioner can inspect those links before deciding whether the finding is useful.</p><figure class="wide issue-figure"><div class="evidence-layout"><div class="font-window"><div class="window-chrome"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>FONT WEBSITE / PURCHASE</span></div><div class="font-shop"><div class="shop-head">Purchase<small>Classic Collection</small></div><div class="font-products"><div class="font-product agatha">Agatha<small>CHF 100</small></div><div class="font-product alaska outlined">Alaska<small>CHF 100</small><span class="target-tag">affected controls ↓</span></div></div><div class="fold"><span>Current viewport</span><span>↓ Scroll to find controls</span></div></div></div><div class="issue-evidence"><div class="mini-label">ISSUE / CONTROLS_NOT_VISIBLE</div><h3>A purchase action that needs finding.</h3><p>Add-to-cart and per-license detail controls require additional scrolling to reach.</p><dl><div><dt>Severity</dt><dd>3 / major usability problem</dd></div><div><dt>Journey step</dt><dd>16</dd></div><div><dt>Next action</dt><dd>Scroll</dd></div></dl><span class="source-label">Reconstruction of the paper example</span></div></div></figure><details class="technical"><summary>Inspect the original interface and provenance<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-body"><p>Each finding can be inspected alongside the think-aloud trace, an annotated screenshot, and a timeline of decisions. The system also includes agent-journey views for page navigation and cognitive intent.</p><a class="original-figure" href="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/Figure-5_interface-example.svg"><img loading="lazy" src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/Figure-5_interface-example.svg" alt="Original font-website figure connecting the issue, edit request, simulation result, and impacted personas" /></a><p class="small">Select the figure to inspect it at full size. This article reconstructs the visible example; its interactive scenes do not run agents.</p></div></details><div class="section-heading" id="refinement"><h2>Move from finding an issue to trying a fix</h2></div><p>Identifying a usability issue is only a first step. UXCascade helps designers propose and explore potential remedies through direct edits or natural-language requests to an HTML snapshot. Here, the proposed fix is to make the purchase action visible on each font card.</p><p>In this example, the request is to place an <strong>Add to Cart</strong> button on each font card. Try the illustrated edit below, then replay the next step.</p><figure class="wide edit-figure"><div class="edit-request"><span class="mini-label">PROPOSE / NATURAL-LANGUAGE EDIT</span><p>“Place an Add to Cart button on each font card.”</p></div><div class="edit-scene" data-edit-state="original"><div class="edit-font"><div class="window-chrome">EDITABLE SNAPSHOT</div><div class="edit-product"><span class="purchase-label">Classic Collection</span><span class="type-specimen">Alaska</span><span class="price">CHF 100</span><span class="added-button">＋ Add to cart</span><div class="edit-cursor">↖</div></div></div><div class="edit-explanation"><span class="mini-label" id="edit-label">ORIGINAL SNAPSHOT</span><h3 id="edit-title">The purchase controls are out of view.</h3><p id="edit-description">Apply the proposed edit to make the action available on the font card.</p><div class="edit-buttons"><button class="primary" type="button" id="apply-edit">Apply proposed edit</button><button type="button" id="replay-step" disabled>Replay next step</button><button type="button" id="reset-edit" class="text-button">Reset</button></div><span class="source-label">Illustrated example · no live simulation</span></div></div><div class="replay-result" hidden aria-live="polite"><div class="action-comparison"><div><span>ORIGINAL NEXT ACTION</span><strong>Scroll</strong></div><b>→</b><div><span>AFTER THE EDIT</span><strong>Click Add to cart for Alaska</strong></div></div><div class="verdict"><span class="action-verdict">Action: different</span><span class="issue-verdict">Issue: remains</span></div><p>The difference report still marks the issue as remaining. A different next action is evidence to inspect; it does not establish that the usability problem is resolved.</p></div></figure><p>The replay keeps the original prompt history and evaluates one next step on the modified snapshot. This makes it a way to probe a local change. It does not establish whether an entire journey improves, or whether a real person would respond in the same way.</p><details class="technical"><summary>What is preserved, and what is re-evaluated?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-body"><p>The Preview Agent receives the modified HTML while retaining the original prompt state. It produces a predicted next action and an assessment of the issue. Existing simulation data can also identify other personas who interacted with the modified element under adjacent goals.</p><p>The preview is local to a step. It does not replay a complete session, establish causal effects on human behavior, or validate a fix with human participants.</p></div></details><div class="section-heading" id="system"><h2>A pipeline that keeps the evidence connected.</h2></div><p>Three groups of agents support this workflow. Browser agents generate persona-grounded interactions. Annotation agents identify goals and possible issues. Refinement agents apply edits and examine a next step.</p><figure class="wide architecture-figure" data-animation><div class="pipeline"><div><b>Simulate</b><span>actions + reasoning</span></div><span class="pipeline-arrow">→</span><div><b>Annotate</b><span>goals + issues</span></div><span class="pipeline-arrow">→</span><div><b>Refine</b><span>edits + replay</span></div></div><div class="snapshot-strip"><span class="mini-label">CONNECTED BY EVENT SNAPSHOTS</span><span>HTML</span><span>Actions</span><span>Memory</span><span>Prompts</span><span>Metadata</span></div><div class="motion-controls"><button type="button" data-motion aria-label="Pause animation" title="Pause animation">Ⅱ</button></div></figure><details class="technical"><summary>Technical implementation and full architecture<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-body"><p><strong>Simulation.</strong> A custom agent built on browser-use follows a perception–decision–action loop. It receives an indexed map of interactive DOM elements and annotated screenshots, and emits structured event snapshots.</p><p><strong>Annotation.</strong> A Tagging Agent labels high-level cognitive intent from reasoning traces. An Issue Detector identifies moments of friction, affected elements, and severity. These outputs support the goals, traits, issues, and journey views.</p><p><strong>Refinement.</strong> An Editor Agent generates DOM-level patches from natural-language instructions. A Preview Agent evaluates the next action and issue state on the changed snapshot.</p><a class="original-figure" href="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/FIgure-5_Agent-framework.svg"><img loading="lazy" src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/FIgure-5_Agent-framework.svg" alt="Full architecture: simulation agents exchange browser state, snapshots feed annotation agents, and refinement agents edit and replay" /></a><p>The full prompt specifications appear in Appendix C of the <a href="/projects/uxcascade/uist26-135.pdf">paper</a>.</p></div></details><div class="section-heading" id="evaluation"><h2>What did UX professionals find useful?</h2></div><div class="annotated-paragraph"><p>Eight UX professionals evaluated a clothing-shop prototype with 17 seeded usability issues. They used UXCascade and a report compiled from ten human testers, with condition order counterbalanced.</p><aside class="margin-note">The evaluation used Cascada Tees, a clothing shop. The font website above illustrates the system workflow; it is not the study task.</aside></div><p>Participants valued the path from goals to traits to issues. It gave them a way to navigate the feedback and inspect individual findings. They also questioned the realism of simulated personas and described an initial learning curve.</p><blockquote><p>“The breakdown by goals and traits makes it much easier to focus compared to the Baseline condition.”</p><cite>P1 · UX professional</cite></blockquote><p>The issue-count comparison is exploratory. Participants found 2.875 seeded issues on average using UXCascade and 2.625 using the baseline report. <strong>The difference was not statistically significant.</strong></p><p>Participants examined the same website repeatedly. Both assisted conditions produced fewer new findings when used second, so order matters when interpreting the results. This comparison does not establish equivalence to human testing.</p><figure class="wide study-figure"><div class="chart-heading"><span class="mini-label">SEEDED ISSUES IDENTIFIED BY PRACTITIONERS</span><div class="segmented" role="group" aria-label="Condition order"><button data-order="overall" type="button" aria-pressed="true">Overall</button><button data-order="first" type="button" aria-pressed="false">Used first</button><button data-order="second" type="button" aria-pressed="false">Used second</button></div></div><div id="study-chart" role="img" aria-label="Mean issues identified: baseline 2.625 plus or minus 0.916 standard deviation, UXCascade 2.875 plus or minus 0.835"><img src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/Figure-6_User-Study-Results.svg" alt="Original study chart with means, standard deviations, and condition order" /></div><div id="chart-mobile-summary" class="chart-mobile-summary"></div><p class="chart-note" id="chart-note" aria-live="polite">Eight participants · Error bars: ±1 SD · Assisted conditions only</p></figure><p>The sources also surfaced different issues. Agent output directly covered 9 of the 17 seeded problems; the human report covered 11. Neither contained everything. These are <em>source-coverage</em> counts, distinct from how many issues practitioners reported while using each source.</p><figure class="wide coverage-figure"><div class="coverage-head"><span>Seeded issue category</span><span>Agent output</span><span>Human report</span></div><div class="coverage-row"><span>Discovery & interaction</span><div><span class="coverage-track"><i style="width:28.571428571428573%"></i></span><b>2/7</b></div><div><span class="coverage-track human"><i style="width:71.42857142857143%"></i></span><b>5/7</b></div></div><div class="coverage-row"><span>Accessibility & communication</span><div><span class="coverage-track"><i style="width:71.42857142857143%"></i></span><b>5/7</b></div><div><span class="coverage-track human"><i style="width:71.42857142857143%"></i></span><b>5/7</b></div></div><div class="coverage-row"><span>Visual design & performance</span><div><span class="coverage-track"><i style="width:66.66666666666667%"></i></span><b>2/3</b></div><div><span class="coverage-track human"><i style="width:33.333333333333336%"></i></span><b>1/3</b></div></div></figure><details class="technical"><summary>Study protocol, statistics, and workload<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-body"><p>Participants first explored the prototype unaided for 7 minutes, then used each assisted condition for 10 minutes. The initial unaided phase produced a mean of 3.750 issues (SD 0.707); it always came first, so it is not an equivalent randomized condition.</p><p>A Wilcoxon signed-rank test of per-participant assisted-condition counts found no significant difference (W = 6, p = .813). With only eight participants and repeated exposure to one website, the findings should be read as exploratory.</p><p>The agents used four binary traits: price sensitivity, time pressure, age cohort, and user type, producing 16 combinations with two agents per combination (32 agents). This is one study configuration, not a validated taxonomy of users.</p><p>No NASA-TLX dimension differed significantly between conditions. Participants described the benefits of structured navigation alongside the effort required to learn the richer interface.</p><a class="original-figure" href="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/Figure_7-NASA-TLX-results.svg"><img loading="lazy" src="/projects/uxcascade/article/assets/Figure_7-NASA-TLX-results.svg" alt="NASA-TLX response distributions for eight participants across six workload dimensions and two conditions" /></a><p>The paper details source-to-issue mapping, coverage, and the qualitative analysis in Section 6.</p></div></details><div class="section-heading" id="outlook"><h2>Simulation is a starting point for investigation.</h2></div><p>The value of UXCascade is the connection between an overview and the evidence behind a finding. A practitioner can move from a pattern, to a particular interaction, to a change worth testing.</p><div class="annotated-paragraph"><p>That connection does not remove the limitations of simulation. Persona labels do not guarantee realistic behavioral differences. Agents can miss visual cues and nuanced human reactions. A single-step replay provides only a small piece of evidence about a design change.</p><aside class="margin-note">Future directions include stronger behavioral grounding, richer visual evidence, full-journey evaluation, and flexible navigation from issues back to goals and personas.</aside></div><p>Better simulations will generate more feedback. The enduring question is how to help people inspect, interpret, and act on that feedback—while retaining the judgment to question it.</p><div class="closing wide"><span class="closing-label">THE CONTRIBUTION</span><p>From many simulated interactions to focused, inspectable findings.</p><div class="read-paper"><a href="/projects/uxcascade/uist26-135.pdf">Read the full paper ↗</a></div></div></div></details></div>
